1. Begin your LaTeX document with the standard preamble:

```
\documentclass{article}

\usepackage{tocloft} % This package provides additional customization options for the TOC

\begin{document}
```

2. Add the `\tableofcontents` command to your document after the `\begin{document}` line:

```
\documentclass{article}

\usepackage{tocloft}

\begin{document}
\tableofcontents
```

3. Add section or chapter headings to your document:

```
\section{First Section}
Some content here.

\section{Second Section}
More content here.

\chapter{First Chapter}
Even more content here.

\chapter{Second Chapter}
And even more content here.
```

4. Compile your document (e.g., using `pdflatex`) to generate the TOC. The first time you compile, LaTeX will create a `.toc` file that stores the TOC information.

5. If you want to customize the appearance of your TOC, you can use commands provided by the `tocloft` package. For example, to change the font size of the TOC entries, you can use:

```
\renewcommand{\cftsecfont}{\large}
\renewcommand{\cftchappresnum}{Chapter \space}
```

Replace `sec` and `chappresnum` with the appropriate ToC list type (e.g., `subsection` or `section`), and adjust the font size to your liking.

6. You can also add a label to the TOC, so that you can reference it from within your document:

```
\label{sec:toc}
\tableofcontents
```

Now you can reference the TOC using `\ref{sec:toc}`.

That's it! Your TOC should now be automatically generated and updated whenever you compile your LaTeX document.

